	(build-host)$ export PATH=$PATH:<PROJECT_DIR>/layers/meta-xilinx/gen-machine-conf/

For Flat Configuration
	(build-host)$ gen-machineconf parse-sdt --hw-description <path_to_dir/my-project-dir> -c conf/ --machine-name versal-vck190-sdt --add-config CONFIG_SUBSYSTEM_MACHINE_NAME=\"versal-vck190-reva-x-ebm-01-reva\"
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME = "versal-vck190-sdt"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

For Segment Configuration
	(build-host)$ gen-machineconf parse-sdt --hw-description <path_to_dir/my-project-dir> -c conf/ --machine-name versal-vck190-sdt-seg --add-config CONFIG_SUBSYSTEM_MACHINE_NAME=\"versal-vck190-reva-x-ebm-01-reva\" -g full
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME = "versal-vck190-sdt-seg"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

NOTE:
If you need to re-run gen-machineconf under an old project, you must clear
the project first:
	(build-host)$ rm conf/machine -Rf; rm output -Rf; rm conf/dts -Rf; rm hw-description -Rf
	(build-host)$ sed -i '/WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME/d' conf/local.conf
And after re-running the gen-machineconf command, don't forget to add
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME back to conf/local.conf according to the type of
XSA file.

7.3 Device Tree Overlay
-----------------------
The Device Tree Overlay(DTO) is used to add the child nodes and the fragments
from the .dtbo file to the base device tree, then triggering drivers to be
probed after PDI programming. So the dtbo file for PL needs to be installed
to rootfs along with PDI files.

7.3.1 SDT Segment Configuration
-------------------------------
The recipe "versal-fw-demo" in the amd-versal bsp layer is used to install
dtbo file to WRLinux by utilizing the bbclass "dfx_user_dts". For a more
detailed description of "dfx_user_dts", please see
	https://github.com/Xilinx/meta-xilinx/blob/rel-v2025.1/docs/README.dfx.user.dts.md

After running the steps in "7.2.3 SDT Workflow", execute the following commands:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cp conf/dts/versal-vmk180-sdt-seg/pl-overlay-full/pl.dtsi <PROJECT_DIR>/layers/amd-versal/recipes-firmware/versal-fw-demo/files
	(build-host)$ cp hw-description/<path_to_pld>/<proj>_pld.pdi <PROJECT_DIR>/layers/amd-versal/recipes-firmware/versal-fw-demo/files
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " versal-fw-demo"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:wr-amd-versal += 'versal-fw-demo'
FW_DEMO_FILES:pn-versal-fw-demo = "pl.dtsi <proj>_pld.pdi"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

The dtbo and pdi files can be found at the boards:
	root@amd-versal:~# ls -l /lib/firmware/xilinx/versal-fw-demo/versal-fw-demo.*
	-rw-r--r-- 1 root root    3825 Apr  5  2011 /lib/firmware/xilinx/versal-fw-demo/versal-fw-demo.dtbo
	-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 1423280 Apr  5  2011 /lib/firmware/xilinx/versal-fw-demo/versal-fw-demo.pdi

And these two files can be used to program PL by following the instructions
in "7.4 PL Programming".

XSCT Segment Configuration
--------------------------
For the XSCT segment configuration, the dtbo file has been installed to
the first partition of the WIC image.
	root@amd-versal:~# ls -l /boot/devicetree/*.dtbo

The *_pld.pdi file can be found at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work/amd_versal-wrs-linux/base-pdi/1.0+2025.1

And these two files can be used to program PL by following the instructions
in "7.4 PL Programming".

7.4 PL Programming
------------------
In AMD Versal platforms, a segmented configuration is split into two
or more PDI files: a smaller "boot PDI" and one or more "PL PDIs". The
boot PDI is loaded first to bring up the processors (CIPS), memory (DDR),
and the Network-on-Chip (NoC). The PL PDI, which contains the programmable
logic configuration, is loaded on WRLinux.

There are 3 methods to program PL from the Linux side:
    - sysfs
    - configfs
    - fpgautil

For a more detailed description of gen-machineconf, please see
	https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/1188397412/Solution+Versal+PL+Programming

7.4.1 Sysfs
-----------
Run the following commands to program PL with the PDI file via sysfs:

	root@amd-versal:~# echo 0 > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/flags
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir -p /lib/firmware
	root@amd-versal:~# cp <path_to_pdi/proj_pld.pdi> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-versal:~# echo proj.pdi > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/firmware

7.4.2 Configfs
--------------
Run the following commands to program PL with the PDI file via configfs:

	root@amd-versal:~# echo 0 > /sys/class/fpga_manager/fpga0/flags
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir -p /lib/firmware
	root@amd-versal:~# cp <path_to_pdi/proj_pld.pdi> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-versal:~# cp <path_to_dtbo/pl.dtbo> /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system
	root@amd-versal:~# cd /lib/firmware/
	root@amd-versal:~# echo pl.dtbo > /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system/path

Steps to remove the drivers got added as part of DTO

	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir /sys/kernel/config/device-tree/overlays/system

7.4.3 Fpgautil
--------------
The fpgautil tool provides a user-friendly interface for loading,
reconfiguring and reading FPGA bitstream configurations. Follow
the steps to install this tool on WRLinux:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'fpga-manager-script'
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " fpga-manager-script"
_EOF

Run the following commands to program PL with the PDI file via fpgautil:
    root@amd-versal:~# fpgautil -b <path_to_pdi/proj_pld.pdi> -o <path_to_dtbo/pl.dtbo>
